body:
@annevk I think origin policy will probably be implemented in the browser in the same place as CORS, so it would still mean re-wiring the handshake to go down the same code path.

The WebSocket handshake started out as purely a mechanism to negotiate a WebSocket connection. Over time we've added HTTP features like cookies and authentication, but we've paid a high price in implementation complexity.

The "right" way to do WebSocket authentication is to do it at the application layer after the handshake completes. No-one asks how to put an oauth token in a TCP/IP SYN packet. But the reality is that we've ended up in a fuzzy middle-ground that is hard to explain.

@ricea 

> The WebSocket handshake security model hinges on exposing no more capabilities for request forgery than are already possible using an img tag. It is not possible to add arbitrary headers to the request using an img tag.

Can you elaborate here. I don't understand why the security model hinges on not allowing headers to be set in the client API? I'm not sure I understand this.

@digitalpacman I'm fully aware. I'm mostly trying to get the implementors to agree we should try to solve this problem. 

> otoh, designs that result in a lot of preflights suck so I'm not convinced we want to enable this rather than pushing it into the post websocket-handshake data.

The problem is that you've left the realm of http auth here and you now have to invent a mini protocol over the websocket itself. You also need to let the "attacker" successfully establish a connection to your server, and you now have to wait for data to be sent over it (which needs to timeout appropriately) to authenticate. So you end up with completely different authentication methods for websocket and non-websockets clients.

@davidfowl Edge is using Chromium, so maybe now this could be implemented?
I fully support what You wrote here: whatwg/websockets#16. The whole idea is to disallow establishing connections for users without auth header and that didn't successfully authenticate.

As I wrote in my initial message, RFC 6455 clearly says that **request MAY include** any other headers, but hey, that's just a standard, we don't have to follow it....or should we 🤔🤔🤔

@domenic @ricea We (Deno folks) are happy to specify and implement extensions outside of the standard (we have previously done this for `fetch`), but that is only possible if the upstream spec provides a logical extension point. Currently the `WebSocket` constructor does not take an options bag, so we can not safely add more arguments. If we could change the HTML spec to have the second argument be a dictionary (with backwards compat for a `sequence<DOMString>` and `DOMString` like it is now), we can add the required extensions outside this spec.

I was thinking something along the lines of this:

```webidl
dictionary WebSocketOptions {
  protocols: sequence<DOMString>,
}

interface WebSocket {
  constructor(USVString url, optional (DOMString or sequence<DOMString> or WebSocketOptions) protocols = []);
}
```

I'd be happy to open a spec changes and write WPTs if this seems reasonable.

In Bun v0.5, we implemented headers support in the `WebSocket` constructor

In TypeScript types form, essentially this:
```ts
declare class WebSocket extends EventTarget {
  constructor(
    url: string,
    options?: { headers?: HeadersInit; protocols?: string[] }
  );
  // ...rest of WebSocket
}
```

This constructor is used when `url` is a string and `options` is an object that is not iterable. The headers used to construct the WebSocket is not exposed to the WebSocket instance (unlike `url`). It's not exposed mostly to minimize differences between the web API version of WebSocket and Bun's implementation.

@benaadams You are painfully correct. I wonder how much this one issue has stunted websocket adoption after all this time 🤔

Like how many people wanted to use websockets and realized how all of their auth middleware just wont work and they have to do some completely different auth mechanism for websockets so they just didn't do it at all.

Imagine if you could literally just send a single header, like all of your other web requests, and boom suddenly you get to have websockets without reinventing auth because your server already does it. What a world that would be! But oh well, a couple of people said it is impossible because of some completely obscure reasons that nobody cares about. Wouldn't want to treat a websocket any different than an img tag, because that makes total sense.

I don't see why it's necessary to find WebSocket servers that currently support CORS preflights. It's quite easy to write a websocket server that adds CORS preflight logic:

```go
g := gin.Default()
g.Use(func(c *gin.Context) {
	c.Writer.Header().Set("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", "*")
	c.Writer.Header().Set("Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "true")
	c.Writer.Header().Set("Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "Authorization, Upgrade")
	c.Writer.Header().Set("Access-Control-Allow-Methods", "OPTIONS, GET")

	if c.Request.Method == "OPTIONS" {
		c.AbortWithStatus(204)
	} else {
		c.Next()
	}
})
g.GET("/ws", func(c *gin.Context){
	conn, _ := websocket.Upgrader{
		ReadBufferSize: 1024,
		WriteBufferSize: 1024,
	}.Upgrade(c.Writer, c.Request, nil)
	conn.SetPingHandler(
		// Handle ping
	)
	// Other business logic here
})
```

> I've got an alternate way to address this, by reusing much of the fetch API and adding a light extension to the streams API.

One benefit of this is that it makes it possible to receive info from failed WebSocket handshake requests.

If the `WebSocket` constructor is modified in order to make it possible to pass a custom HTTP headers (such as `Authorization` and [`DPoP`](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449)), someone will very quickly <del>complain that it is not possible</del> ask to receive the `WWW-Authenticate` header from failed requests in order to know:

* which authentication schemes are available;
* which [error](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6750#section-3.1) happened;
* which [DPoP algorithms and nonce](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-7.1) must be used;
* which [`resource` URI](https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-ietf-oauth-resource-metadata-01.html#name-www-authenticate-response) must be used.

Allowing passing `Authorization` is only half of the solution.

> Although it is attractive to re-use the `RequestInit` options for the WebSocketStream constructor, many combinations of options aren't really defined for WebSockets and it would take a lot of analysis before we could feel confident enabling them all.

@ricea That's fair. And I myself have some general ergonomic hesitations with this API, and almost stated that in the initial comment posting it. (The `fetch` API is just generally awkward for duplex request streams.)

> > I've got an alternate way to address this, by reusing much of the fetch API and adding a light extension to the streams API.
> 
> One benefit of this is that it makes it possible to receive info from failed WebSocket handshake requests.

@randomstuff That was part of my motivation for this, actually. It offers a standard API that can expose that info.

Edit: syntax

Gave this a second thought. Here's the current stuff from `RequestInit` that apply to WebSocket responses and upgradable connections in general:

- `body`
- `credentials`
- `headers`
- `mode`
- `priority`
- `redirect`
- `signal`

The following options could make sense in theory, but would see almost zero use in practice with upgradable requests:

- `attributionReporting`
- `browsingTopics`
- `referrer`
- `referrerPolicy`

The following options have no applicability at all to upgradable requests:

- `cache`
- `integrity`
- `keepalive`
- `method`
- `duplex`

Also, clients are supposed to *not* send messages until after the connection begins, per RFC (see page 19 of https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455.html). So the current API doesn't actually gain anything by buffering messages.

And finally, there's no easy way for websocket instances to reliably filter out text or binary messages when it can only process the other type, despite there being a dedicated response code for that.

I wonder if using a `Request` subtype would achieve a better result?

```webidl
enum WebSocketMessageType {"text", "binary"};

typedef (USVString or WebSocketRequest) WebSocketRequestInfo;

// Abort the readable stream with this or an `WebSocketAbortError` object to close the stream client-side with that code + reason combo
dictionary WebSocketAbortErrorInit {
  unsigned short closeCode = 1000;
  // max length of 125 bytes as consistent with its UTF-8 encoding
  USVString closeReason = "";
};

interface WebSocketAbortError : DOMException {
  constructor(optional DOMString message = "", optional WebSocketAbortErrorInit options = {});
  readonly attribute unsigned short closeCode;
  readonly attribute USVString closeReason;
};

dictionary WebSocketRequestInit {
  HeadersInit headers;
  ReadableStream? body;
  USVString referrer;
  ReferrerPolicy referrerPolicy;
  RequestMode mode;
  RequestCredentials credentials;
  RequestRedirect redirect;
  AbortSignal? signal;
  RequestPriority priority;
  BinaryType binaryType;
  sequence<DOMString> acceptedMessageTypes = ["text", "binary"];
  sequence<DOMString> protocol = [];
  // sequence<DOMString> extensions = [];
};

interface WebSocketRequest : Request {
  // Requires a body, but could be supplied by either parameter
  constructor(WebSocketRequestInfo input, optional WebSocketRequestInit init = {});
  readonly attribute BinaryType binaryType;
  readonly attribute sequence<DOMString> acceptedMessageTypes;
  readonly attribute sequence<DOMString> protocol;
  readonly attribute sequence<DOMString> extensions;
};

// Returned from `fetch` if given a `WebSocketRequest`
interface WebSocketResponse : Response {
  readonly attribute boolean upgraded;
  readonly attribute sequence<DOMString> protocol;
  readonly attribute sequence<DOMString> extensions;
};

partial interface mixin WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope {
  [NewObject] Promise<WebSocketResponse> fetch(WebSocketRequest input, optional WebSocketRequestInit init = {});

  // Like `fetch` but coerces URLs to `WebSocketRequest`s instead
  [NewObject] Promise<WebSocketResponse> openWebSocket(WebSocketRequestInfo input, optional WebSocketRequestInit init = {});
};
```

The spec-internal `mode: "websocket"` would be changed to an internal "upgrade" value that may be `null` or `"websocket"`. That way, CORS can work as usual. Patching the Fetch spec for this is incredibly straightforward, and I expect implementations to also be similarly easily updated to match. And since it's a subtype, it could be easily diverted by type alone.

CORS preflights for websocket fetches would include three special headers for WebSocket upgrade `Access-Control-Request-Upgrade` for HTTP upgrades in general and `Access-Control-Request-Protocol` to check if use of the given protocol is allowed. Absent matching `Access-Control-Allow-Upgrades` and `Access-Control-Allow-Protocols` headers in the response, CORS-guarded WebSocket connections would not be allowed.

This also leaves the door open on deferring support for specific fetch options. If you aren't yet comfortable with how to do CORS, you can just drop the `mode` and `credentials` properties for now. If you aren't yet comfortable exposing referrer information, you could drop `referrer` and `referrerPolicy` for now.

It's also easily extended to support other types of persistent connections derived from HTTP requests, like server-sent events ([which currently *do* already have a userland implementation](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@microsoft/fetch-event-source)).

@ricea Does this structure work better? It should hopefully 1. be more future-proof and 2. be easier to incrementally adopt.

Deno 2.5 [adds support](https://deno.com/blog/v2.5#websocket-headers) for setting headers in the WebSocket constructor:
```ts
const ws = new WebSocket("wss://api.example.com/socket", {
  headers: new Headers({
    "Authorization": `Bearer ${token}`,
    "X-Custom": "value",
  }),
});
```
